 -----------\n\n3:30pm - 4:00pm\nIN PERSON\nNew Faces Speaker: Thomas Fitzp
 atrick\, MD\nActing Instructor\, Department of Medicine\, Division of Alle
 rgy & Infectious Diseases\nTalk Title: Perspectives of People Who Use Drug
 s on the Risks and Benefits of Anti-Fentanyl Immunotherapies and Clinical 
 Trial Participation\nBio: Thomas (Tom) Fitzpatrick\, MD\, MPH\, is a board
 -certified internal medicine and infectious disease physician and Acting I
 nstructor in the UW Division of Allergy and Infectious Diseases. His overa
 rching goal as a clinician and researcher is to improve the health of comm
 unities disproportionately affected by infectious diseases and substance u
 se disorders by developing new interventions that reduce harms from higher
 -risk behaviors. He has two decades of experience implementing and evaluat
 ing public health programs in partnership with people who use drugs (PWUD)
  in the United States and China. During his clinical training\, he was awa
 rded several research fellowships and grants\, including the Doris Duke In
 ternational Clinical Research Fellowship\, the Boston University Fellow Im
 mersion Training Program in Addiction Medicine\, the UW ADAI Small Grant P
 rogram\, the China Medical Board Postdoctoral Research Fellowship\, and th
 e Fordham HIV and Drug Abuse Prevention Research Ethics Training Fellowshi
 p.\n \n4:00pm - 5:00pm\nIN PERSON\nCFAR Seminar Speak\n\nEvent URL: https:
 //washington.zoom.us/meeting/register/JeKyeA5wTWuN34fxcSzHdw\n\nSource: uw
 _cfar
